    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR (17.9%) diverges >30% from revenue 5yr CAGR (12.1%). Book value compounds on ~75% earnings retention and margin expansion (EBITDA margin 33% -> 37%), not on a repeatable growth rate; revenue is the more honest forward proxy, so it is selected. EPS 5yr (26.2%) starts from a tax-depressed FY2021.",
    "notes": "FCF CAGRs are on the owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award taxes). Owner-FCF CAGR is ABOVE reported: SBC (~2% of revenue) grew slower than cash flow, so SBC is not eating the growth. The 3yr FCF CAGR starts from a FY2023 working-capital trough (inventory build post Philips recall) and overstates the run-rate."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "ResMed delivers its FY2027 guide ($5.75-5.85B; the low reported number is the MatrixCare divestiture plus FX, not a demand stall) and then grows high-single digits as the under-diagnosed OSA pool, the AirSense 11 replacement cycle and the masks/accessories annuity on a growing installed base compound. GLP-1 proves net neutral-to-positive (ResMed's 2.5M linked-patient data), Philips rebuilds US share only slowly under its consent decree, and guided gross-margin expansion lifts adj. EBITDA margin ~170bp over five years before plateauing. Growth fades toward GDP-plus by year 10.",
    "bull": "GLP-1 becomes an outright funnel: physician screening of GLP-1 patients raises sleep-apnea diagnosis rates and CPAP initiation/compliance, while Philips stays a marginal US device supplier for years. Revenue runs ~9% in FY28-29 on top of the guided FY27 top end, the post-MatrixCare focus and mask mix take adj. EBITDA margin to ~42.5%, and the market keeps paying a 22x quality multiple at exit.",
    "bear": "Philips resolves its consent decree and re-enters the US with discounting, CMS competitive bidding and prior-authorization trim reimbursement, Astral ventilator headwinds persist, and sustained population-level GLP-1 weight loss reduces moderate OSA prevalence and new-patient device demand over the decade. Growth settles near 3%, price and mix compress adj. EBITDA margin from 38.8% to 36%, and the exit multiple de-rates to 15x as the market stops treating ResMed as a compounder."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "ResMed is valued as a mature, cash-generative device-plus-consumables franchise on a strict owner-FCF basis (SBC and equity-award taxes expensed, count held flat). The valuation turns on two external axes - Philips' US re-entry and GLP-1's long-run effect on sleep-apnea prevalence - which are carried in the scenario paths and a 25% bear weight rather than in the discount rate.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Philips Respironics re-entering the US device market with price after its consent decree",
      "Long-run GLP-1 weight loss reducing OSA prevalence and new-patient device demand",
      "Medicare competitive bidding / prior authorization compressing device and resupply reimbursement",
      "Terminal value is ~60-70% of EV: the 20x cap multiple and 3% terminal growth carry most of the value",
      "FX: ~42% of revenue outside the Americas"
    ]
  },
